  "content": {
    "id": "p68-range52-59-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the Cartesian equation of the following, stating any necessary restrictions.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p68-q52",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(\\sec t,\\tan^2t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=x^2-1$, where $x\\le-1$ or $x\\ge1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\tan^2t\\\\y&=\\sec^2t-1\\\\y&=x^2-1\\end{align*}$$Since $x=\\sec t$, $x\\le-1$ or $x\\ge1$. The parameter excludes $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi$, $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q53",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$(\\sec^2t,\\tan^2t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=x-1$, where $x\\ge1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\tan^2t\\\\y&=\\sec^2t-1\\\\y&=x-1\\end{align*}$$Since $\\sec^2t\\ge1$, $x\\ge1$. Equality occurs at $t=0$, giving $(1,0)$. The parameter excludes $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi$, $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q54",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(\\log_2(t),\\log_2(\\frac{4}{t}))$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2-x$, where $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
          "worked": "Both logarithms require $t>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\log_2(4/t)\\\\y&=\\log_2 4-\\log_2 t\\\\y&=2-x\\end{align*}$$As $t$ ranges over the positive reals, $x=\\log_2t$ ranges over all real numbers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q55",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$(\\sin^2t,\\cos^2t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=1-x$, where $0\\le x\\le1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\cos^2t\\\\y&=1-\\sin^2t\\\\y&=1-x\\end{align*}$$Since $x=\\sin^2t$, $0\\le x\\le1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q56",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$(\\cos t,2\\cos^2t-1)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2x^2-1$, where $-1\\le x\\le1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=2\\cos^2t-1\\\\y&=2x^2-1\\end{align*}$$Since $x=\\cos t$, $-1\\le x\\le1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q57",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$(3^t-1,9^t-1)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=x^2+2x$, where $x>-1$.",
          "worked": "Since $3^t=x+1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=9^t-1\\\\y&=(3^t)^2-1\\\\y&=(x+1)^2-1\\\\y&=x^2+2x\\end{align*}$$Since $3^t>0$, $x>-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q58",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$(\\sqrt{1-2t},1+t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x^2+2y=3$, where $x\\ge0$.",
          "worked": "The square root requires $t\\le\\frac12$ and gives $x\\ge0$. Since $t=y-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=1-2t\\\\x^2&=1-2(y-1)\\\\x^2&=3-2y\\\\x^2+2y&=3\\end{align*}$$The Cartesian restriction is $x\\ge0$. The corresponding y-range is $y\\le\\frac32$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q59",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$(2-3\\cos t,\\sin^2t)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=1-\\dfrac{(2-x)^2}{9}$, where $-1\\le x\\le5$.",
          "worked": "From $x=2-3\\cos t$, $\\cos t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\sin^2t\\\\y&=1-\\cos^2t\\\\y&=1-\\left(\\frac{2-x}{3}\\right)^2\\\\y&=1-\\frac{(2-x)^2}{9}\\end{align*}$$Since $-1\\le\\cos t\\le1$, the x-coordinate satisfies $-1\\le x\\le5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      }
    ]
  },
